:synohw_dx_plug:
> **Where it comes from.** The separate expansion plugin (synology-expansion, formerly synology-dx) ships the `synology_dx` kernel module (source `synology-dx/`, README there). It reads the GPIO pins of the DX1215's master port multiplier (the 88SM9705 with EMID 0) through libata and exposes them in `/sys/kernel/synology_dx/unit/` (`fan_level`, `fan_code`, `fan1_fail`, `fan2_fail`, `power_on`, `psu1`, `psu2`, `switch_mode`, `model`, `unique`, `master_host`, `last_error`) and as hwmon `synology_dx` (`fan1_alarm`, `fan2_alarm`, `pwm1`). The bit meanings are from Synology's own code (`research/2026-10-25-dx1215-gpio-spec.md`, page table lines 86-88) and were measured on this unit (`experiments/2026-10-25-synology-dx-phase2a/RESULT.md`, `-phase2b/RESULT.md`).
>
> **What is known for sure.** The fan level reads back what was written (each write 0.05 s); low is the CPLD's power-on default and the lowest level at which the fans run; stop stops both fans and sets both alarm bits within 5 s. **Not known:** which value of psu1 / psu2 means "OK" (the DX1215 has one PSU; both read 0); shown raw. **Verified at the box (2026-10-26):** switch_mode 1 = the rear switch on DEFAULT. There is no RPM and no temperature sensor in the DX1215: its disks' temperatures come from Unraid like every other disk.
>
> **Cost of reading.** A unit value older than the module's cache (30 s) makes the module re-read the port multiplier: one short libata error-handler round on the DX master port, during which that port's I/O waits. The daemon reads once per `DX_PERIOD` (60 s) and saves a copy in `/var/run/synology-hardware/dx`; this page shows that copy while the daemon runs and reads the module itself only when it does not.
>
> **Without the module** nothing here can see or change the DX1215; the daemon writes nothing, and the DX1215 keeps its level (low after power-on).
:end

